<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
HTTP proxies built on <code>http-proxy</code>, such as <code>http-proxy-middleware</code> and the
Vite dev-server and preview-server proxies, accept a <code>secure</code> option. Setting it to
<code>false</code> disables TLS certificate validation for the connection to the proxied target,
so a man-in-the-middle can intercept or modify the proxied traffic.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Do not set <code>secure: false</code>. If the target uses a self-signed certificate, trust that
certificate explicitly (for example with the <code>ca</code> option or the
<code>NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS</code> environment variable) instead of disabling validation.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following Vite configuration disables certificate validation for the proxied backend:
</p>
<sample language="javascript">export default defineConfig({
  server: {
    proxy: {
      '/api': { target: 'https://localhost:7095', secure: false }
    }
  }
});</sample>
<p>
Remove the <code>secure: false</code> option so that the certificate of the target is validated.
</p>
</example>

<references>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/295.html">CWE-295</a>.</li>
<li>Vite: <a href="https://vite.dev/config/server-options.html#server-proxy">server.proxy</a>.</li>
<li>http-proxy: <a href="https://github.com/http-party/node-http-proxy#options">Options</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
